## Requirements

Pick one toolchain:

* **Visual Studio 2022** with the "Desktop development with C++" workload, plus
  CMake (bundled with Visual Studio), or
* **MinGW-w64** (`g++`, `mingw32-make`, `windres`) plus GNU Make.

## Build

Visual Studio / MSVC:

```bat
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --config Release
build\Release\{{ bin_name }}.exe
```

MinGW-w64:

```sh
make            # -> build/{{ bin_name }}.exe
make run
{% if is_console %}
make test
{% endif %}
make clean
```

MakeMaker can drive the CMake recipe for you:

```sh
makemaker build .
makemaker run .
```

## Layout

```
{{ slug }}/
├── CMakeLists.txt        build config (MSVC and MinGW)
├── Makefile              MinGW-only build
├── src/
│   ├── app.h             shared declarations
│   ├── app.{{ source_ext }}             application core
{% if is_gui %}
│   ├── winmain.cpp       Win32 window + entry point
│   ├── app.rc            version info + embedded manifest
│   ├── resource.h        resource identifiers
│   └── {{ class_name }}.manifest   DPI awareness + common controls
{% else %}
│   └── main.{{ source_ext }}            entry point
└── tests/
    └── test_app.{{ source_ext }}        unit tests (make test)
{% endif %}
```
{% if is_gui %}

The GUI flavour is plain Win32 (no MFC, no ATL): a window class, a
`WM_PAINT` handler and a message loop. `app.rc` embeds the version resource and
the application manifest, which is what makes the window per-monitor DPI aware.
{% endif %}
